<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hikone Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Hikone</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Ii family on Lake Biwa, pillars of the Tokugawa order whose head Ii Naosuke opened Japan to Western trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a>, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu's</a> most trusted generals and leader of the red-armoured troops, was given the former lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawayama-castle">Sawayama</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600. After his death in 1602 his sons built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikone">Hikone</a> castle nearby, completed in 1622.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ii, hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Tokugawa with up to 350,000 koku, guarded the approach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and supplied several great elders (tairo) to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke">Ii Naosuke</a>, tairo from 1858, signed the treaty with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> without imperial approval and purged opponents in the Ansei purge.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke">Naosuke</a> was assassinated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakuradamon-incident">Sakurada Gate</a> in 1860 and the domain was reduced. In 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikone">Hikone</a> switched to the imperial side. After abolition in 1871 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a>; Hikone castle is a national treasure.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hikone-domain.jpg" alt="Hikone Domain" width="320" height="173"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hikone_Castle_oldphoto_omotemon.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hikone</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ii Naomasa, Ii Naotaka, Ii Naosuke</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hikone han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=hikone-domain" aria-label="Hikone Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8129 902 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hikone Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8449 1158L8530 1145M8449 1158L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8449 1158h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8449 1158h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hikone. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=hikone-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
